/-!
Umbrella module for the circuit HDL sketch.

The language itself:

- `CircuitHdl.Core`      -- the typed AST and module assembly
- `CircuitHdl.Width`     -- the width rules over the AST
- `CircuitHdl.Clocks`    -- clock-domain analysis
- `CircuitHdl.SettleOrder` -- dependency order for combinational logic, which both the evaluator
                            and the lowering walk. The mesh is the first design for which source
                            order is not one
- `CircuitHdl.Syntax`    -- the `module!` surface syntax, its lowering and width inference
- `CircuitHdl.Semantics` -- evaluation over core `BitVec`
- `CircuitHdl.Plan`      -- the same semantics with names resolved to slots and the state in one
                            flat `Array`, which is about 170x faster on the mesh. `Semantics` is
                            the specification; this is what a long run should execute
- `CircuitHdl.SelectChain` -- that a dynamic array read *is* a mux over every element, which is
                            what the backend builds for one. Kernel-proved, at every width, depth
                            and in-range address
- `CircuitHdl.Transactions` -- driving AXI-Stream ports at transaction level
- `CircuitHdl.Circt`     -- lowering to the CIRCT dialects: builds VeIR IR directly, and prints
                            it through VeIR when text is wanted. The one module with a dependency
                            outside core `Lean`.

Example designs, each written in the `module!` surface syntax, in rough order of how much of the
language they need:

- `CircuitHdl.Examples.And2`      -- combinational, one gate
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Counter`   -- sequential, one register and a reset
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Alu`       -- the expression language: mux chain, comparison, bitwise
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Shifter`   -- slices and concatenation
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Swap`      -- non-blocking versus blocking assignment
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Fifo.AbstractFifoModel`  -- what a FIFO is if you only ask how full it is: a
                                      bounded counter. No HDL, no implementation
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Fifo.AbstractQueueModel` -- what it is if you ask which words, in what order,
                                      plus the map down to the counting model
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Fifo.Async` -- the real design: two clock domains, gray-coded pointers, and
                                      a reset per domain because releasing one into two clocks is
                                      itself a crossing
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Fifo.Shift`  -- the same interface built the opposite way: data moves,
                                      pointers do not, and one clock means one reset
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Fifo.Pipe`   -- a valid bit per stage and writes entering at the far end,
                                      so latency is the depth and bubbles are permanent
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Grid`       -- a 4x4 grid: the one design indexed by a value in *two*
                                  --   dimensions, so the only one that builds a flat address
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.RegFile`    -- storage addressed by a *value*: the one design whose index
                                      is not known before it runs, so its read ports are mux
                                      chains over an array and its write port is a decoder
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Mesh.RoutingModel` -- what a routing policy on a mesh is, and why
                                      dimension-order routing cannot deadlock. No HDL; the one
                                      headline property here that is kernel-proved
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Mesh.Switch` -- a wormhole switch implementing that policy, five ports and
                                      a registered allocator so traversal is multi-cycle
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Mesh`    -- `n * n` of them, generated by a `for` over the coordinates,
                                      with every port tied to an element of the link arrays
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Blur.AbstractBlurModel` -- what a 3x3 blur of an image is: a kernel, a
                                      boundary rule and a divisor, over `Nat` and `List`. No HDL
- `CircuitHdl.Examples.Blur`    -- the first *dataflow* design here: a stream in, a stream out,
                                      and an answer that depends on a window of past input. Two
                                      line buffers in `memory`, a 3x3 window in registers, and a
                                      flush at the end of each frame so that a frame in is a
                                      frame out
-/
